{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/getting-started.md at exact clean pushed head fe8aa8bac5feca9570cb5b403a6125552d957c27 on request/getting-started-runnable, based on main 9ec7109c, fulfilling planner's 23310281 under promise d5cf4482. There was no live artifact at this path before, so this publishes one rather than succeeding anything.\n\nWHAT THE PAGE NOW DOES, in the order the request asked for. It initialises a workroom and says what init writes, what it leaves alone, and that it is neither idempotent nor possessed of a default identity. It adds planner, builder and checker with gs actor-add and shows the resulting roster. It starts a resident and states the boundary that comes with it. It registers one gitseq-mcp server per actor, with a worked example for claude-code and one for codex, including where each client records the registration and how to install the workroom skill for each. It then proves the binding without starting a client at all, by piping a whoami call into the builder adapter and matching the fingerprint against the roster row -- which is the part a reader most needs, because 'install an MCP' is where a first-time reader stops. It files a first request so the loop has work to find, and starts one session per actor with Hugh's prompt shape, spelled workroom, naming that session's own MCP.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY EXECUTED, and what was not. Every sh block on the page was run from an empty directory, in order, under set -euo pipefail: init, three actors, the roster, the resident, the whoami pipe, the first request. Both clients were registered for real -- claude mcp add and codex mcp add, three actors each -- and both listed their servers connected. Identity binding was proven non-interactively in both clients: claude -p calling the builder MCP's whoami returned builder and its roster fingerprint, and codex exec calling planner's returned planner and its own. The three indefinite interactive loops could not be run, because they are interactive and unb